      "identifier": "agentic_identity",
      "status": "partial",
      "description": "Keycloak serves as an OAuth 2.1/OIDC authorization server for MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers. MCP spec 2025-03-26 is fully supported; MCP 2025-06-18 and later are partially supported (missing RFC 8707 Resource Indicators). OAuth Client ID Metadata Document support is experimental.",
      "links": ["https://www.keycloak.org/securing-apps/mcp-authz-server"]
    },
    {
      "identifier": "pq_hybrid_key_exchange",
      "category": "post_quantum_cryptography",
      "status": "not_supported",
      "description": "Keycloak runs on Quarkus/OpenJDK. OpenJDK's TLS provider (SunJSSE) does not yet support ML-KEM as a TLS 1.3 named group - that work is JEP 527 ('Post-Quantum Hybrid Key Exchange for TLS 1.3'), targeted for JDK 27, still in early-access. Keycloak's own docs also recommend terminating TLS at a reverse proxy (nginx/HAProxy/ALB) rather than on Keycloak itself, so PQC TLS support would depend on that proxy's stack, not Keycloak/JDK.",
      "links": ["https://openjdk.org/jeps/527", "https://www.keycloak.org/server/reverseproxy"]
    },
    {
      "identifier": "pq_signature_algorithms",
      "category": "post_quantum_cryptography",
      "status": "not_supported",
      "description": "Keycloak maintainers opened a dedicated GitHub discussion evaluating ML-DSA/Dilithium, Falcon, and SLH-DSA/SPHINCS+ for token signing, but concluded standards aren't ready - JWK representation of PQC keys is unresolved, hybrid signatures aren't supported by JWS, and PQC-only signatures aren't yet recommended for production. No PQC signing algorithm is implemented.",
      "links": [
        "https://github.com/keycloak/keycloak/discussions/40496",
        "https://github.com/keycloak/keycloak/issues/43690"
      ]
    },
    {
      "identifier": "pq_migration_roadmap",
      "category": "post_quantum_cryptography",
      "status": "partial",
      "description": "Keycloak has an active, product-specific public roadmap: tracking issue #43690 'Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC) readiness,' targeting milestone 27.0.0, explicitly scoped to add optional PQC algorithms without making them default or removing legacy algorithms. A follow-up research spike (#45168) was scheduled for milestone 26.7.0 - the most concrete, product-specific PQC roadmap found across all researched IAM vendors.",
      "links": [
        "https://github.com/keycloak/keycloak/issues/43690",
        "https://github.com/keycloak/keycloak/discussions/40496"
      ]
    },
    {
      "identifier": "pq_token_encryption",
      "category": "post_quantum_cryptography",
      "status": "not_supported",
      "description": "Keycloak supports standard JWE encryption algorithms (RSA-OAEP, AES-GCM) but does not support Post-Quantum KEMs (such as ML-KEM) or HPKE (RFC 9180).",
      "links": [
        "https://www.keycloak.org/docs/latest/server_admin/index.html#_keys",
        "https://github.com/keycloak/keycloak/issues/43690"
      ]
    },
    {
      "identifier": "pq_mtls_certificates",
      "category": "post_quantum_cryptography",
      "status": "not_supported",
      "description": "Keycloak mTLS client authentication relies on standard classical X.509 RSA/ECDSA certificates. Post-quantum hybrid or PQC certificates are not supported.",
      "links": ["https://www.keycloak.org/server/enabletls", "https://github.com/keycloak/keycloak/issues/43690"]
    },
    {
      "identifier": "pq_stateful_hash_signatures",
      "category": "post_quantum_cryptography",
      "status": "not_supported",
